# Licence and rules for the contact data

Everything under `packages/contacts/data/` is available under the
**Open Database License (ODbL) v1.0** — https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/odbl/1-0/

Share it, adapt it, build on it; attribute Siyar contributors, keep any
redistributed database under ODbL, and do not use technical measures to restrict
others' access to it.

The data comes in **two tiers, and they are deliberately not merged.**

---

## Tier 1 — `directory.json` (curated, verified)

The strict tier. A record may be added only if it is an address the
organisation has **itself published for the purpose of receiving job
applications** — a `careers@` / `jobs@` / `hr@` address printed on the
organisation's own careers page or official posting.

Every record must carry:

- `sourceUrl` — the organisation's **own** page where the address appears. CI
  rejects a source whose host does not match the address domain, so an
  aggregator or job board is not an acceptable source.
- `lastVerified` — the date the source was last checked.

Never included in this tier:

- Personal mailboxes of named individuals.
- Addresses obtained by crawling, scraping, leaks, or purchase.
- Addresses taken from a third party's paid product.

This tier is what makes the guarantee mean anything. Diluting it would make
every record in it worthless, which is the whole reason tier 2 exists
separately.

## Tier 2 — `bundled/*.json` (third-party, unverified)

Compiled lists contributed in bulk. They **cannot** meet the tier 1 standard —
there is no per-record published source — so they are kept apart, marked
`"verified": false`, and labelled as unverified everywhere they appear in the
product.

Each list must carry:

- `provenance` — where the list came from, in plain words, in both languages.
- `verified: false` — enforced by the schema. There is no way to mark a bundled
  list verified; a list that earns that belongs in tier 1 instead.
- `approximateVintage` where known, because stale contact data is the norm and
  users deserve to know.

Per-entry `flags` warn about what the data actually is:

- `hiring` — the local part names a recruitment mailbox. The only positive signal.
- `free-webmail` — a Gmail/Hotmail-style address, very often an individual's.
- `legacy-domain` — a domain belonging to a defunct or consolidated ISP; likely
  dead, and possibly re-registered by someone unrelated.

**Understand what tier 2 is.** These are, in the main, general company contact
addresses — `info@`, `sales@`, a managing director's office — not recruitment
mailboxes. Some belong to named individuals. Writing to them with a job
application is unsolicited mail to a person who did not ask for it, a large
share will bounce, and bounces at volume damage the sender's own mailbox
reputation. The product says so before anyone uses one; contributors and forks
should not quietly remove that warning.

CI enforces that the two tiers stay disjoint and that no suppressed address
appears in either.

---

## Removal

Any organisation or individual may have an address removed, from either tier,
with no questions asked and no justification required. See `REMOVALS.md`.

Removed addresses go to `suppression.json` as SHA-256 hashes, which the sender
always subtracts and which CI checks both tiers against — so a removal survives
a later bulk import that happens to contain the same address.
